; The bench driver's contract: a number is printed only inside a timing window,
; and a build beside it is refused before anything is measured. The test runs
; the driver itself, with `env -i` and only the variables a case names, so a
; name the caller exported cannot decide what the case proves. Most cases stop
; at a refusal before a measurement; the last three go past the window with the
; explicit opt-out or with a window that is open, and stop at the next thing
; the driver needs — a binary and a corpus — so the opt-out is proved to reach
; the rest of the checks rather than only the window message. Those three
; answer both halves of the driver's build check with something the test owns:
; an empty slots directory it names and removes, and a `ps` of its own, because
; the slot half reads a directory while the process half reads the host's whole
; process table. None of them measures anything, so the test needs no corpus
; and no build of its own.
;
; capabilities.t runs the built driver's request pass on fixtures/unit.jazz,
; so the binary and the fixture are dependencies: a rebuilt driver re-runs it.

(cram
 (deps ../../bench/run.sh ../../bench/bench.exe fixtures/unit.jazz))
